WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:03.670
Human judgment is powerful, but it is not perfectly consistent.

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:07.000
Cognitive biases are predictable tendencies that can influence reasoning.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.099
Noise is unwanted variation in judgments that should be consistent.

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:12.080
Bias pushes judgment in a pattern.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.490
Noise makes judgment scatter.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.390
Both can damage decision quality,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.992
especially when teams assume that experience alone makes their conclusions reliable.

00:00:18.992 --> 00:00:22.890
Confirmation bias leads people to favor evidence that supports what they already believe.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.860
Anchoring makes the first number, idea, or proposal exert too much influence.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.765
Availability makes recent or vivid examples feel more common than they are.

00:00:30.765 --> 00:00:33.535
Overconfidence makes people underestimate uncertainty.

00:00:33.535 --> 00:00:37.220
Sunk cost makes it hard to stop investing in a weak path because time, money,

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:39.110
or reputation has already been committed.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:40.720
Group settings create additional risk.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.920
Groupthink can make people suppress concerns to preserve harmony or avoid conflict.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.419
A senior person can unintentionally anchor the room.

00:00:47.419 --> 00:00:49.680
A team can confuse confidence with evidence.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.970
A fast-moving culture can reward decisive language while quietly punishing uncertainty.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.620
Decision hygiene is the set of habits that helps reduce these avoidable errors.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.760
Useful practices include checklists, explicit assumptions, independent estimates,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.435
pre-mortems, red-team review at a high level, and structured disagreement.

00:01:05.435 --> 00:01:09.475
A pre-mortem imagines that the decision failed in the future and asks what likely caused

00:01:09.475 --> 00:01:10.165
the failure.

00:01:10.165 --> 00:01:12.570
This makes it easier to surface risks before commitment,

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.390
when the team can still change the plan.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:17.120
Decision hygiene does not assume perfect rationality.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.730
It assumes people are busy, social, emotional, and operating under pressure.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:21.785
Structure helps judgment.

00:01:21.785 --> 00:01:23.855
It creates space for evidence, dissent,

00:01:23.855 --> 00:01:27.185
and review without requiring everyone to become detached or flawless.

00:01:27.185 --> 00:01:30.480
The point is not to remove human judgment, but to support it.
